EXTERNAL chose(knot)

// [draft]
=== saudi_station ===
This is Saudi coffee: lightly roasted beans, cardamom and saffron. Watch and learn. #lana
Lana… is this coffee or milky tea? It's yellow! #salma #smile
What's wrong with its colour? Yours is black as coal, and did I say a word? #lana #angry
…It's good. But don't you dare say “told you.” #salma #thinking
I didn't say anything. My face did. #lana #smile
-> END

// [draft]
=== majlis_bench ===
Oh my… it's like my mother's house in Jeddah. Her [[majlis]] looked just like this. #tourist #smile
You're most welcome, Auntie. Shall I pour you some Saudi coffee? #lana
Yes please, dear, and a few dates. Amman always did know how to make a person feel at home. #tourist
And this window… it hasn't changed. #tourist #thinking
-> END

// [draft]
=== menu_board ===
Okay, quick meeting… I mean, a chat. What goes first on the board? #salma #thinking
Saudi. Even in the Arabic alphabet, Saudi's first letter comes before Ammani's. #lana #smile
* [“Ammani first. We're in Amman.” #ammani]
    Fine, Ammani on top… but Saudi in bigger letters. #lana
* [“All right, Saudi first. Guests come first.” #saudi]
    Guest? I own half this place, cousin. But fine, I accept. #lana #smile
-
Girls, the board doesn't drink. What matters is what's in the cup. #abu_fadi
-> END

// [draft]
=== dates_bowl ===
My mother messaged. #lana #surprised
Tell me, is the café doing well? Are the dallahs still on the shelf? And don't tell anyone I asked. #nawal
Twenty years, she hasn't asked about a thing here… and she says don't tell anyone. And I told you. #lana #sad
Tell her they're all on the shelf. And the little one's back too. #salma #smile
And I'll tell her you put dates on every table, the way we do back home. #lana #smile
-> END

// [draft]
=== cardamom_jars ===
A jar for cardamom, a jar for saffron… did Teta label everything? #lana
Everything, except what was in her heart. #salma #sad
You know what? Mama's become like that too. I've got used to her silence… it's her voice I miss. #salma #sad
Mine's the same. She calls me every day and says nothing. #lana #sad
Come on, let's fill the jars. At least these can be filled. #salma
-> END

// [draft]
=== two_cups ===
Two matching cups on the counter. One for me, and one for you. #salma #smile
And then what? We split the cups down the middle, like the ledger? #lana
No. I want to cross out the line down the middle of the ledger. One ledger, for one café. #salma
Fine. But I keep the accounts. Nobody can read your writing. #lana #smile
Deal. And look what I found in the recipe book: “For Nawal's wedding, 1997. Two coffees in one cup.” #salma
My mother's wedding? Teta made her a coffee from two cities… #lana #surprised
-> END

// [draft]
=== m5_lead ===
Your Teta roasted the beans by hand, in the kitchen out back. You could smell it at the end of the street. #abu_fadi
And she had two jars. One she opened every day… the other, I never once saw her open. #abu_fadi #thinking
-> END

// [draft]
=== m5_intro ===
Im Samira, the first roast in my own café! But the kitchen's still upside down. #teta_young #smile
Never mind, I'll look with you. What do you need right now? #samira_mother_1974
Everything! Quick, my heart, before the beans burn. #teta_young
-> END

// [draft]
=== m5_perfect ===
Bless your hands! Not one bean burned. #teta_young #smile
-> END

// [draft]
=== m5_ok ===
A little burnt? Never mind. Roasting is like raising children: a low fire and patience. #teta_young
-> END

// [draft]
=== m5_outro ===
Why two jars, Im Khalil? It's the same coffee. #samira_mother_1974
This one's for the house, every day. And on this one I'll write: “For later.” #teta_young
And when is this “later”? #samira_mother_1974 #thinking
The day when some things can't be said. On that day, the cup will do the talking. #teta_young
-> END

// [draft]
=== m5_return ===
Found it! At the back of the shelf: “For later,” in Teta's writing. #salma #surprised
There's no coffee in it… just a folded paper, with my name on it. #lana #surprised
Lana held the paper for a while, then put it in the counter drawer, beside Teta's first letter. #sys
-> END

// [draft]
=== letter_3 ===
Lana, my heart, Nawal's girl, #letter
When your mother was little, she was the quietest one in the house. She'd sit behind the counter, hear everything, and say nothing. #letter
She kept a secret the way she kept her own name. Not once did she betray anyone. #letter
But your mother keeps promises too well, my heart. #letter
And the promise she carries, I'm the one who… #letter_faded
If she goes quiet, sit beside her. With Nawal, silence is love, not anger. #letter
— Your Teta #letter_signature
-> END

// [draft]
=== voice_3 ===
Lana, my heart… if you've found my letter, don't ask your mother what happened. Ask her how she is, and she'll tell you herself. #teta
-> END

// [draft]
=== notify_3 ===
I asked Mom how she is, like Teta said. She went quiet for a bit… then she asked about Aunt Rana. #lana
-> END
